\[
U=k\frac{q_1q_2}{r}
\]
\[
U=\frac{(8.99\times10^9)(2.0\times10^{-9})(-5.0\times10^{-9})}{0.50}
\]
\[
U=-1.8\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm{J}
\]
The energy is negative because the charges have opposite signs.

The total electric potential energy is the sum over interacting pairs:
\[
U_{\mathrm{tot}}=
k\frac{q_1q_2}{r_{12}}
+k\frac{q_1q_3}{r_{13}}
+k\frac{q_2q_3}{r_{23}}
\]
There is no single-charge term because potential energy belongs to interactions between charges after a reference is chosen. An isolated point charge has no pair separation with another charge in this model.
